<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schmalkaldic War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1546">1546</a>–<a href="/years/1547">1547</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Schmalkaldic War of 1546–1547 was fought between Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> of Lutheran princes and cities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Allied with the Protestant Duke Maurice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, Charles defeated and captured Elector John Frederick of Saxony at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a> in 1547. The electoral title and much territory passed to Maurice, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> survived and was recognised in 1555.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war.jpg" alt="Schmalkaldic War" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlos_V_en_la_Batalla_de_M%C3%BChlberg,_por_Tiziano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Many imperial states adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> despite the opposition of the Catholic House of Habsburg, which saw the conversions as a quest for autonomy at the expense of imperial authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> of 1521 placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> under the imperial ban. Lutheran states led by Elector John Frederick I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Landgrave Philip I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkalden">Schmalkalden</a> in 1531.</p><p>In 1544 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after the Treaty of Crépy and sought allies, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a> and the Lutheran Duke Maurice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, cousin of John Frederick. On 4 July 1546 the League's leaders met at Ichtershausen and decided on a preventive war. Luther had died in February 1546; he had argued against the legality of such a war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> in July 1546, when the League's army occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fussen">Füssen</a>. The League would not give battle against the entrenched imperial troops, and William IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Archduke Ferdinand I declared themselves neutral. On 20 July Charles placed John Frederick and Philip under the imperial ban. In October Maurice, helped by Ferdinand, invaded his cousin's Ernestine lands, and John Frederick turned back to retake them.</p><p>Meanwhile the imperial cities and princes of the south, among them Duke Ulrich of Württemberg, submitted to the emperor. In 1547 Charles went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, where he joined his brother Ferdinand, and on 24 April the League's forces were routed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a>, where John Frederick was taken prisoner. Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> held out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>John Frederick was sentenced to death and, to obtain a pardon, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-of-wittenberg">Capitulation of Wittenberg</a> on 19 May 1547. He lost the electoral dignity to Maurice, who was declared elector on 4 June. Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> surrendered and was imprisoned at once.</p><p>In 1548 Charles issued the Augsburg Interim to prepare the return of Protestants to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. It provoked a revolt in 1552, the Second Schmalkaldic War, led by Maurice with the backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a>. Charles fled and cancelled the Interim in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-passau">Peace of Passau</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> followed three years later, and the next year Charles abdicated in favour of Ferdinand.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Schmalkaldic War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The battle decided the <b>Schmalkaldic War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-24">24 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1547 Ferdinand I punished the towns for refusing aid in the <b>Schmalkaldic War</b>, confiscating lands and privileges.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusatian-league">Lusatian League</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Schmalkaldic War</b> he eventually sided with Charles V.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-brandenburg-kustrin">Margraviate of Brandenburg-Küstrin</a></li></ul></section>
